;; ===========================================================================
;; RENDER
;; ===========================================================================
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 ; THE PROJECTION CONTRACT, generic (owner direction, 2026-07-27 night).
 ; A unit is any map that declares, per OUTPUT KIND, the fully qualified
 ; symbol of the function that projects it into that kind. One router
 ; resolves and applies. The render contract's `:seon.render/ai` +
 ; `:seon.render/html` pair (docs/seon/architecture/ui.md, "The block and
 ; its two renders") is the SPECIAL CASE of this, not a separate
 ; mechanism; new kinds — log, sms, metrics — are accretion: add a key,
 ; write the fn, no router change and no registration table.
 ; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

 ; A kind IS its key: `:seon.render/ai` is both the declaration key on a
 ; unit and the kind a caller asks for. Nothing maps short names to keys,
 ; because a second naming is a second place to drift.
 :seon.render/kind :qualified-keyword
 :seon.render/kinds [:set :seon.render/kind]
 ; The viewer namespace whose `render-<kind>` defn may override a matching
 ; schema's default. Explicit request data, never ambient `*ns*`.
 :seon.render/namespace :symbol
 ; The declaration. USUALLY a fully qualified symbol naming the
 ; projection fn, resolved LATE on every render so a re-`defn` takes
 ; effect with no re-registration — the proven `:seon.source/populate`
 ; / `:seon.cluster.loop/evaluate` / predicate-owner idiom.
 ;
 ; A SYMBOL, and only a symbol — because this schema is what the two
 ; kind attributes are declared as, and a kind attribute is STORED. One
 ; Datahike value type stores natively (`:db.type/symbol`) where a union
 ; needs a pr-str codec on every read and write, which is the codec the
 ; quarry had and this design deleted.
 ;
 ; Proven the hard way rather than reasoned about: widening this to
 ; `[:or :qualified-symbol :string [:vector :any]]` made the bridge
 ; derive `:db.type/string` from the union's first branch and every
 ; block transaction refused. The durable/runtime split is not a
 ; preference — the schema IS the durable side.
 :seon.render/projection :qualified-symbol

 ; The RUNTIME affordance, which is deliberately not an attribute: a
 ; unit built in memory may declare a literal that is its own output —
 ; a verbatim string for a prose kind, a vector for a hiccup kind
 ; (docs/seon/architecture/ui.md, "The block and its two renders"). This
 ; is the accretion `seon.render`'s docstring scoped and deferred out of
 ; step 1: a block that just says a fixed thing should not have to
 ; define a function to say it.
 ;
 ; `seon.render/declaration?` is the code-level contract that admits
 ; both, and the admissible SHAPES are narrow on purpose: `kinds`
 ; derives what a unit can become from the unit itself, so every shape
 ; admitted becomes a shape that turns an ordinary
 ; `seon.render`-namespaced key into a KIND. `:seon.render/priority 3`
 ; is presentation data and must stay data, so a number, keyword, map
 ; or set declares nothing.
 :seon.render/literal [:or :string [:vector :any]]

 ; `:any` twice, at this router's one genuinely polymorphic boundary and
 ; for the same reason value admission gives: IN, a unit is any map any
 ; owner declares (naming a shape here would defeat the mechanism — the
 ; whole point is that a family the router has never heard of routes);
 ; OUT, the projection's grammar belongs to the KIND'S CONSUMER — prose
 ; for `ai`, a line for `log`, hiccup for `html` — and restating it here
 ; would be a second contract that drifts from the consumer's.
 ; The keys are `:qualified-keyword` rather than `:any`, because fully
 ; namespaced map keys are the house rule and this is where it is
 ; enforceable for free.
 :seon.render/unit [:map-of :qualified-keyword :any]
 :seon.render/output :any

 :seon.render/request
 [:map {:closed true}
  [:seon.render/unit :seon.render/unit]
  [:seon.render/kind :seon.render/kind]]
 ; The output is WRAPPED, and that is deliberate: an unwrapped return
 ; would be indistinguishable from a flat `:seon.error/value` for any
 ; projection whose own output is a map. With the wrap, "did this
 ; render?" is `:seon.error/kind` presence, by construction.
 :seon.render/rendered [:map {:closed true} [:seon.render/kind :seon.render/kind] [:seon.render/output :seon.render/output]]

 ; The two kinds the error family declares today. Registered here, in the
 ; router's own file, because a kind is a contract between the router and
 ; a consumer rather than a property of any one producer — and because
 ; the loader is global, an owner declaring a new kind adds one line to
 ; whichever file owns that consumer.
 :seon.render/ai :seon.render/projection
 :seon.render/log :seon.render/projection

